course: adult-life
lesson: 38
title: Looking after yourself and a home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you grow up and leave home, nobody else cooks, cleans, washes your clothes, pays the bills or sends you to bed. You have to do it all yourself.
explain: Grown-ups learn these jobs slowly, often by getting them wrong first, and the ones who practise as children find it much easier.
explain: Vivid example: in Melbourne, cousin Jess moves into her first rented flat with a bag of old plates from Gran. In her first week she turns all her white shirts pink, eats toast for tea three nights running and rings her mum to ask how to boil an egg.
explain: Looking after yourself and a home means cooking, cleaning, laundry, shopping on a budget, paying bills, renting, finding a doctor, sleeping enough, being on time, making scary phone calls, reading what you sign and knowing when to call an expert.
fact: Knowing a few simple meals saves money and keeps you healthy; adults who cannot cook spend much more on takeaways and ready meals.
fact: A home does not clean itself: dishes, hoovering, the bathroom and the bins all need doing, and when you live alone it is all your job.
fact: Laundry means sorting colours, reading labels and choosing the right temperature; a red sock in a white wash turns everything pink.
fact: A budget is the amount of money set aside for something, like food for the week; putting things back on the shelf is a way to stay within it.
fact: Bills are regular payments for electricity, gas, water, the internet, phones and council tax; paid late, they bring extra charges and services can be cut off.
fact: Renting means paying a landlord every month to live in a home that belongs to someone else; renters usually pay a deposit at the start and sign an agreement.
fact: A GP is a family doctor; in the UK you sign up with a local practice, and people must do it again themselves when they move town.
fact: Looking after yourself means sleeping enough, eating properly, moving your body and resting; grown-ups have nobody to send them to bed, so they must decide for themselves.
fact: Being often late can lose someone a job or upset friends, because it says other people's time does not matter.
fact: Lots of young adults find phone calls to strangers scary; planning what to say first helps, and it gets easier with practice.
fact: A contract is a written promise, for a phone, a flat or a job; the small print is the detail, and once you sign you usually have to keep to it.
fact: Never sign something you do not understand; asking questions first is what sensible adults do.
fact: Some home jobs, especially with water, gas and electricity, are too tricky or dangerous to do yourself, so grown-ups call and pay an expert like a plumber.
exercise: Dad makes a tasty dinner from just a few things in the fridge. Why is that a useful grown-up skill? | It saves money and keeps you healthy | It means you never need to shop again | It is only useful for chefs at work | It makes the fridge last longer
exercise: Saturday morning, Mum says, "Right, everyone, cleaning time." Why does this have to happen? | A home does not clean itself | The council checks houses every week | Cleaning is only for parties | Dust disappears by itself after a month
exercise: When you live on your own, who does the cleaning? | You do | Your landlord does | The council does | Your old school does
exercise: Dad holds up a pink shirt that used to be white. What went wrong? | He washed it with something red | He ironed it too hot | He left it out in the sun | He used too little soap
exercise: Mum puts things back on the shelf at the supermarket. Why? | To stay within her food budget | Because the shop is closing | Because they are too heavy to carry | Because the shelf looked empty
exercise: What is a budget? | The money set aside for something, like a week's food | A special kind of shopping trolley | A bill sent by the bank every year to every family | The money left over after the holidays
exercise: Dad sighs at an envelope: "The electricity bill's come." What is a bill? | A regular payment for things like power and water | A present from the electricity company at Christmas time | A letter saying the power is broken | A fine for using too many lights
exercise: What happens if bills are not paid on time? | Extra charges are added and services can be cut off | Nothing at all, they are just a friendly reminder to pay | The bill is cancelled after a week | The government pays it instead
exercise: Your big cousin moves into her first flat that belongs to a landlord. What is she doing? | Renting it and paying him every month | Buying it with one payment | Living there for free as a guest of the family | Looking after it while he is away
exercise: After moving town, your cousin says, "I need to register with a GP." What is a GP? | A family doctor at a local practice | A kind of driving licence | A shop that sells medicine without a prescription | A teacher for grown-ups
exercise: Mum says, "I'm going to bed early, I need to look after myself." Why does she have to decide this herself? | Grown-ups have nobody to send them to bed | The doctor tells her every night | Her boss checks when she sleeps | Children are in charge of bedtime
exercise: Dad grabs the car keys: "If we leave now we won't be late." Why does being on time matter so much for adults? | Being late a lot can lose jobs and upset friends | Shops lock their doors after just one minute | The police fine people who arrive late to anything | Clocks only work if you are on time
exercise: Your big brother paces up and down before ringing to book a haircut. What is going on? | Phone calls to strangers make him nervous | He doesn't know what a haircut is | He is trying to save his phone battery | He is waiting for the hairdresser to ring
exercise: What helps most with a scary phone call? | Planning what to say first | Hanging up as soon as they answer | Ringing at midnight when it's quiet | Asking a friend to shout it for you
exercise: Mum reads every page before signing: "Always read the small print." What is the small print? | The detail of a contract you are agreeing to | The part that doesn't count at all | A secret code that only lawyers and judges can read | The page numbers at the bottom
exercise: Dad says on the phone, "I'll need to get a plumber in." Why not just do it himself? | Some jobs, especially with water, are tricky or risky | Plumbers are free for everyone | It is against the law for anyone but a plumber to touch a tap | He wants someone to talk to
test: Jess, in her first flat: "Toast for tea again lol, can't afford Deliveroo every night." What skill would help her most? | Cooking a few simple cheap meals | Ordering bigger takeaways less often | Buying a much bigger toaster | Moving back home to Gran's
test: Your little brother says, "When you grow up the mess just goes away by itself." Put him right. | No, someone has to clean, and alone it's you | Yes, grown-up homes somehow stay clean on their own | Yes, the landlord comes round to tidy up | No, grown-ups just never make a mess
test: Mum, laughing: "Who put a red sock in with the school shirts?!" What is the lesson? | Sort laundry by colour before washing | Never wash school shirts at all | Red socks are not allowed at school | Wash everything together to save time
test: Dad at the checkout: "Put the crisps back, mate, we're over this week." What is Dad doing? | Keeping to the weekly food budget | Saying crisps are bad for you forever | Punishing you for something you did | Saving the crisps for a party
test: Overheard: "Bugger, forgot the gas bill, now there's a late fee." What has happened? | The bill wasn't paid on time, so extra was charged | The gas company sent a present by mistake | The gas has run out because it's been so cold this winter | The bill was paid twice by accident
test: Cousin on the phone: "Landlord wants a month's deposit before I get the keys." What is a deposit here? | Money paid at the start of renting | The price of buying the whole flat | A tip for the landlord's kindness | A fee for cutting the extra keys
test: Big sister: "Moved to Glasgow, got flu, and I'm not even with a doctor up here. Nightmare." What did she forget? | To register with a local GP | To bring her old doctor with her | To buy a doctor's uniform | To ring her old school nurse
test: Dad, yawning at midnight on his laptop: "Nobody's gonna make me go to bed, are they." What does this show? | Adults must choose good sleep for themselves | Adults never need much sleep once they are grown | Children decide when adults sleep | Laptops help you sleep better
test: Your mate says, "Being late's no biggie, my uncle's late for work every day." What might happen to the uncle? | He could lose his job | His boss will give him a pay rise | Nothing ever, late is fine at work | He will be made the manager
test: Big brother, about to sign a phone contract: "Dunno what half this says, just gonna sign it." What would a sensible adult do? | Ask questions until he understands, then decide | Sign quickly before the deal goes away | Sign with a made-up name to be safe | Let the shop assistant sign it for him
test: Dad, staring at a sparking plug socket: "Don't touch it, I'm ringing an electrician." Why is he right? | Electricity mistakes can be dangerous, so call an expert | Electricians are cheaper than doing it yourself | Plug sockets are owned by the government | He just doesn't like using tools
exam: Ascertain the consequence most probably incurred by a householder who fails to remit payment of a utility bill by the date stipulated. | Additional charges, and possible disconnection of the service | Automatic cancellation of the outstanding sum | Payment of the sum by the local authority on his behalf | A complimentary extension of the service
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the arrangement whereby a person occupies a dwelling owned by another in exchange for monthly payments. | Renting | Purchasing | Inheriting | Guest accommodation
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a prudent adult examines the small print of a contract prior to signature. | Once signed, the terms are usually binding upon the signatory | The small print is customarily of no legal effect whatsoever | Signatures are invalid unless every page is read aloud first | The small print contains the signatory's own address
exam: Whereas a householder discovers a defect in the domestic water supply beyond his competence, determine the course of action consistent with this lesson. | Engage and pay a qualified tradesperson, such as a plumber | Attempt the repair regardless of the hazard | Leave the defect unattended indefinitely | Request that the children undertake the repair
